This repository contains the preprocessed data and analysis code accompanying the study “Effects of Short-Term Breathwork on Respiration and Cognitive Performance 
in a Discrimination Task” by Lena Hehemann, Lisa Stetza, and Christoph Kayser, Department of Cognitive Neuroscience, Bielefeld University.
The study investigates how brief, one-minute periods of structured breathing affect respiration and subsequent cognitive performance 
in a visual emotion discrimination task. Across two experiments, participants performed different breathing practices that manipulated either 
respiratory frequency or the inhalation-to-exhalation ratio before completing the task.

Experiment 1 compared slow breathing (box breathing) with fast breathing, 
while Experiment 2 compared short-inspiration/long-expiration (SILE), long-inspiration/short-expiration (LISE),
and a no-intervention (NOI) condition involving spontaneous breathing.

Repository contents
The repository provides preprocessed and derived data together with the scripts used to reproduce the analyses reported in the manuscript.
The analysis code was developed and tested using:
MATLAB R2022b
Statistics and Machine Learning Toolbox
Other MATLAB versions may also work, but the code has been tested with MATLAB R2022b.

The provided data include, where applicable:
-preprocessed respiratory measures
-derived measures of respiratory frequency and inhalation-to-exhalation ratio
-measures of atypical or undefined respiratory cycles
-behavioral measures, including reaction times and response accuracy
-condition- and time-resolved data used for the statistical analyses

The analysis scripts operate on these processed data and are used to:
-visualize respiratory and behavioral measures
-perform the statistical analyses reported in the manuscript
-analyze respiratory dynamics during and following the breathing interventions
-analyze temporal changes in reaction times and response accuracy
-generate the figures and statistical results presented in the manuscript

Raw physiological recordings are not provided in this repository. 
The repository therefore focuses on the processed data necessary to reproduce the reported analyses and visualizations.

Study design
Respiratory data were originally recorded using a thermistor-based system and analyzed with respect to respiratory frequency, 
inhalation-to-exhalation ratio, respiratory phase, and atypical respiratory cycles. Behavioral analyses focused on reaction times 
and fraction of correct responses in the subsequent emotion discrimination task.

To run the provided MATLAB code, follow these steps:
1. Download the required files and Toolboxes
Download Analyse_data.m and data.mat and store both files in the same folder.
Download bayesFToolbox (Source code is available at: https://github.com/klabhub/bayesFactor)
Download cameri colormap (https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/68546-crameri-perceptually-uniform-scientific-colormaps/files/crameri)
or change the colormap to one of your choice (line 96)
2.Set the data path
Open Analyse_data.m in MATLAB and change the path in line 7 to the folder containing Analyse_data.m and data.mat. and all other dowmloads
3.Add the custom functions
Download the custom_functions folder and add its location to the MATLAB path.
4.Run the analysis
Open Analyse_data.m in MATLAB and run the script. The script will process the provided data,
perform the statistical analyses, and generate the corresponding visualizations and results.

Reproducibility
The analysis scripts are intended to allow the statistical analyses and visualizations 
reported in the manuscript to be reproduced from the provided processed data.

Ethics and data protection
The study was approved by the ethics committee of Bielefeld University,
and all participants provided written informed consent.
The data provided in this repository have been processed and prepared for sharing in 
accordance with the applicable ethical and data-protection requirements.

Citation
If you use the data or code provided in this repository, please cite the associated publication:
Hehemann, L., Stetza, L., & Kayser, C. (2026) Effects of Short-Term Breathwork on Respiration and Cognitive Performance in a Discrimination Task.

